ately you get this type  of stress ting graph structures if I  chose random vertices I get this more  nature organic feel to them and so what  are the takeaways from our project well  I would say that yes we can quantify her  maps there are certain defining  characteristic Eric turistic that there  are in the maps I wouldn't say that  they're fractal it's a little  disappointing to her and me but I think  it's I think it was interesting to try  to come up with some of these metrics  that we could investigate I  I'm not just a mathematician I am also a  mathematical artist so it's a lot of fun  to make these new maps choosing points  is a hard thing to do or choosing where  you want your blocks and I love  generative art also  so Emily's takeaways were that it was  really fascinating to see a statistical  analysis of her  it makes her think about her art a  little bit differently which you know if  you go into if you if he is that going  to change the way she makes art in the  future well she'll probably be informed  a little bit by what we were talking  about the art was more random than she  expected she she was going in and  thinking I'm doing this very  algorithmically but when you look at  some of these things there's more  randomness than she expected in terms of  the process and then the paper and then  the humidity and then the the all the  the tools that she was using and a  philosophical question is when you do  this with a piece of art and you learn  about it from a quantitative point of  view are you really taking the art out  of the art right this is now super  numerical and it's not artistic or is it  is this just a different layer or what  what's happening there so this is a  philosophical question there's lots of  things that we'd like to learn more  about but what I can say is if you're  interested in seeing these slides as you  saw you can go to my website and  download these slides and if you're  interested in seeing me as a an artist I  have my ar